<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siraf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siraf was a port on the Iranian coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, near modern Bandar Taheri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a> province. In the 9th and 10th centuries it was the Gulf's leading harbour, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After an earthquake in 977 and political changes it declined, and its trade passed to the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>. Excavations in the 1960s and 1970s uncovered its mosque, houses and harbour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siraf.jpg" alt="Siraf" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrSamma · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siraf-Wharf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
